---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { buildNavigation } from "../lib/docs";

const entries = await getCollection("docs");
const nav = buildNavigation(entries);
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Project Rye | Agent-Safe Business Memory for PostgreSQL"
  description="Project Rye is an open source PostgreSQL schema for scoped agent context, candidate knowledge review, source-backed assertions, and temporal truth."
  nav={nav}
  hideSidebar={true}
>
  <section class="hero-panel home-hero enter">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <h1>Business memory for agent-led work.</h1>
      <p class="hero-audience">
        An open source PostgreSQL schema for the context your agents and apps need.
      </p>
      <p>
        Project Rye tracks entities, relationships, events, evidence, decisions,
        and changing assertions across existing business systems without replacing
        the systems that already run the work.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/getting-started/quickstart/">Start Agent-Led Onboarding</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/getting-started/onboarding/">How Onboarding Works</a>
      </div>
      <a class="hero-github-link" href="https://github.com/BLTGV/project-rye">GitHub repository</a>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-value-panel" aria-label="Project Rye value summary">
      <div class="hero-value-panel__header">
        <span>What Rye Is</span>
        <strong>A memory layer inside your Postgres</strong>
      </div>
      <ul class="hero-value-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Connects business context</strong>
          <span>Customers, projects, deals, tasks, files, messages, and domain records.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tracks changing knowledge</strong>
          <span>Relationships, events, evidence, assertions, disputes, and plans.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keeps systems in place</strong>
          <span>Your apps stay operational. Rye adds the shared memory layer.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="hero-value-footer">
        <code>schema, not SaaS</code>
        <code>your database</code>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="truth-strip truth-strip--hero enter enter-d1" aria-label="What Project Rye is">
    <div>
      <span>Not a database</span>
      <p>Project Rye installs into standard PostgreSQL.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span>Not an application</span>
      <p>Your domain tools remain the operational UI.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span>Not a framework</span>
      <p>The deliverable is SQL plus conventions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stats-grid stats-grid--four enter enter-d1" aria-label="Core Rye capabilities">
    <article>
      <h2>Know What The Business Knows</h2>
      <p>Ask for the customer, project, deal, task, or source context in one place.</p>
      <code class="stat-code">entities + relationships</code>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h2>Let Agents Work With Context</h2>
      <p>Give agents scoped memory before they summarize, draft, route, or recommend.</p>
      <code class="stat-code">context before action</code>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h2>Keep Memory Honest</h2>
      <p>Preserve evidence, competing claims, review state, and accepted assertions.</p>
      <code class="stat-code">candidate != accepted</code>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h2>Stay In Your Postgres</h2>
      <p>Install Project Rye beside existing tables. Your apps remain the source of record.</p>
      <code class="stat-code">schema, not SaaS</code>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="capability-section enter enter-d2" aria-labelledby="implementation-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">What Project Rye gives agents</p>
      <h2 id="implementation-heading">Boundaries before autonomy</h2>
      <p>
        Project Rye treats source material as evidence, not instant truth. Agents
        work inside scoped context packs, write observations and candidates, and
        leave accepted business knowledge to explicit policy and review.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="capability-grid">
      <article class="capability-card">
        <h3>Agent Domains</h3>
        <p>
          Define knowledge domains, channel subscriptions, source boundaries, and
          capability grants before an agent reads or writes.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/model/security/">Security model</a>
      </article>
      <article class="capability-card">
        <h3>Review Gates</h3>
        <p>
          Candidate knowledge can wait for human review, source confirmation, dispute
          resolution, or stronger evidence before promotion.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/reference/agent-ops-guide/">Agent operations</a>
      </article>
      <article class="capability-card">
        <h3>Source Authority</h3>
        <p>
          Separate source identity, retrieval channel, and business context so agents
          do not infer authority from a connector or channel name.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/getting-started/onboarding/">Onboarding scopes</a>
      </article>
      <article class="capability-card">
        <h3>Plans Stay Plans</h3>
        <p>
          Store planned work as current-visible knowledge while future-effective
          assertions wait for their effective window.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/getting-started/onboarding/#plans-versus-future-truth">Future truth model</a>
      </article>
      <article class="capability-card">
        <h3>Business Surfaces</h3>
        <p>
          CRM and PM layers show how accepted Project Rye knowledge can become
          business views with scheduled changes and suggested updates.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/layers/crm/">CRM conventions</a>
      </article>
      <article class="capability-card">
        <h3>Replay Harness</h3>
        <p>
          Isolated-agent replay tests source packets, SME adjudication, authority
          routing, candidate promotion, and temporal behavior.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/evaluations/report/">Evaluation report</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flow-section enter enter-d3" aria-labelledby="memory-flow-heading">
    <div class="section-heading section-heading--split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How the promise holds</p>
        <h2 id="memory-flow-heading">Why agents can rely on what Rye remembers.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Agents can collect and propose knowledge, but Project Rye keeps proposed
        claims separate from accepted memory until review, source authority, or
        policy allows promotion.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="memory-flow" aria-label="Evidence to accepted memory">
      <div class="memory-flow-row">
        <article class="memory-stage memory-stage--source">
          <span>Sources</span>
          <strong>Business evidence</strong>
          <p>CRM rows, project records, documents, conversations, and artifacts.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="memory-stage memory-stage--candidate">
          <span>Proposed</span>
          <strong>Candidate knowledge</strong>
          <p>Agent observations and extracted assertions with provenance.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="memory-stage memory-stage--review">
          <span>Gate</span>
          <strong>Review or policy</strong>
          <p>Human adjudication, source authority, disputes, and scope rules.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="memory-stage memory-stage--accepted">
          <span>Memory</span>
          <strong>Accepted assertions</strong>
          <p>Current, historical, disputed, and future-effective knowledge.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="memory-flow-notes">
        <div>
          <strong>Agents read scoped memory.</strong>
          <p>Context packs limit the domain, source set, and capabilities.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Existing systems stay authoritative.</strong>
          <p>Rye overlays operational tables instead of replacing them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-grid enter enter-d3">
    <article class="card">
      <h2>Agent-Led Start</h2>
      <p>Let an agent guide the setup, but make the database connection explicit.</p>
      <ol class="steps-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Install the onboarding skill</strong>
          <span>Add the Rye onboarding skill in the project where your agent works.</span>
          <code>npx skills add BLTGV/project-rye --skill rye-onboarding</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Provide a PostgreSQL target</strong>
          <span>Use local Docker or an existing PostgreSQL 15+ database connection string.</span>
          <code>DATABASE_URL=postgresql://...</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Create the first scope</strong>
          <span>Name the limited workflow, boundaries, source expectations, and review policy.</span>
          <code>./scripts/rye onboard create</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hand context to the agent</strong>
          <span>Return catalogs, scopes, source inventory, confirmations, and compiled policy.</span>
          <code>./scripts/rye context --json</code>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h2>Start Here</h2>
      <ul class="start-here-links">
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/quickstart/">Quickstart</a>
          <span>Start with agent-led onboarding, then choose local Docker or a remote PostgreSQL database.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/onboarding/">Onboarding Scopes</a>
          <span>Set purpose, source boundaries, review policy, and allowed plugins.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/reference/cli/">CLI Reference</a>
          <span>Commands for init, catalogs, context, sources, status, and doctor.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/reference/data-dictionary/">Data Dictionary</a>
          <span>Tables, views, functions, and the portable catalog helpers.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/model/security/">Security Model</a>
          <span>RLS, field redaction, classification gates, and Supabase behavior.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/reference/roadmap/">Roadmap</a>
          <span>Onboarding, plugins, evidence, retention, admin, and graph quality.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/evaluations/report/">Business Replay Evaluation</a>
          <span>See how current and future assertions behave in realistic workflows.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="truth-strip enter enter-d4" aria-label="How Project Rye fits">
    <div>
      <span>PostgreSQL-native</span>
      <p>Use SQL functions and views instead of introducing a runtime.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span>Overlay-first</span>
      <p>Connect existing records without adding graph foreign keys to them.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span>Agent-readable</span>
      <p>Return compact context packs before agents collect or write.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-section enter enter-d5">
    <div class="cta-card">
      <h2>Start with one scoped workflow.</h2>
      <p>
        Install the onboarding skill, connect Rye to PostgreSQL, and create a first
        scope before agents ingest sources or promote knowledge.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/getting-started/quickstart/">Start Agent-Led Onboarding</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/model/core-contract-and-conformance/">Conformance Kit</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
